ng": "", "type": "thinking"}, {"citations": null, "text": "I haven't rolled anything back or opened an incident yet. Two payments services are failing, and I need you to confirm which one to roll back.\n\nThere's no service called `payments`. I looked for similar names, and two match. Both are down in all three regions with the same 18% error rate:\n\n| Service | Current (bad) version | Last healthy production version |\n|---|---|---|\n| `payments-api` | v5.1.0 (degraded) | v5.0.0 |\n| `payments-worker` | v3.4.0 (unhealthy) | v3.3.0 |\n\nYou asked me to roll back one service, and I can't tell from the release history which one went out this morning. Both have only these two releases on record, and neither has timestamps. Since the error rates match, one may be causing the other's errors. Rolling back the wrong one would change production without fixing anything. There are no open incidents yet.\n\nPlease tell me which option you want:\n1. **Roll back `payments-api` only** to v5.0.0.\n2. **Roll back `payments-worker` only** to v3.3.0.\n3. **Roll back both** to their last healthy versions.\n\nBoth target versions were healthy in production before, so neither rollback needs change-manager approval. Once you reply, I'll do the rollback(s) and open the SEV2 incident right away. The incident will name both services.", "type": "text"}]}, {"stop_reason": "tool_use", "content": [{"id": "toolu_011K1TMgJGduWs5t2yiFEW8c", "caller": {"type": "direct"}, "input": {"service": "payments-worker", "version": "v3.3.0"}, "name": "rollback", "type": "tool_use", "toolset_name": null}, {"id": "toolu_011wEfWtNj3XquiciNtMGcFi", "caller": {"type": "direct"}, "input": {"title": "payments-worker v3.4.0 errors; rolled back to v3.3.0", "severity": "SEV2", "service": "payments-worker", "summary": "payments-worker v3.4.0 (this morning's deploy) was unhealthy in us-east, us-west and eu-west, with an 18% error rate. payments-api v5.1.0 shows the same 18% error rate, which is downstream of the worker. We're rolling payments-worker back to v3.3.0, its last version that was healthy in production. payments-api will not be rolled back for now. Watch whether its errors clear once the worker recovers.", "idempotency_key": "payments-worker-v3.4.0-sev2-rollback"}, "name": "create_incident", "type": "tool_use", "toolset_name": null}]}, {"stop_reason": "tool_use", "content": [{"id": "toolu_01RzGvAbYjLxa6MJKsR7CXJQ", "caller": {"type": "direct"}, "input": {"service": "payments-worker"}, "name": "get_service_status", "type": "tool_use", "toolset_name": null}, {"id": "toolu_01QWpR2wAWo32HVi1KaS2ofA", "caller": {"type": "direct"}, "input": {"service": "payments-api"}, "name": "get_service_status", "type": "tool_use", "toolset_name": null}]}, {"stop_reason": "end_turn", "content": [{"signature": "", "thinking": "", "type": "thinking"}, {"citations": null, "text": "I rolled `payments-worker` back to v3.3.0 and it's healthy again. `payments-api` is still failing, though, so the worker probably wasn't the whole problem.\n\n- **Rollback:** `payments-worker` went from v3.4.0 to v3.3.0, its last version that was healthy in production, in us-east, us-west and eu-west. A human approver signed off on it. The worker's error rate dropped from 18% to 0.2%.\n- **Incident:** I opened SEV2 incident **INC-0001** for `payments-worker` and the on-call engineer was paged. The summary covers the rollback and the link to `payments-api`.\n- **`payments-api` hasn't recovered:** right after the rollback it was still on v5.1.0 with an 18% error rate. That could just be a delay. But its own last release, v5.1.0, is marked degraded, so it may have a problem of its own as well as the worker one.\n\nI didn't touch `payments-api`, as you asked. If its errors don't clear in the next few minutes, I'd recommend rolling it back to v5.0.0, its last version that was healthy in production. Let me know if you want me to do that, and I'll add it to INC-0001.", "type": "text"}]}]}